{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'cat clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/full-trace/trace-packet-r3.json'","aggregated_output":"[\n {\n  \"point\": 1,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"Micron (as reported by the author)\",\n   \"passage\": \"tight market conditions could last until 2028\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Micron (memory supplier)\",\n    \"what\": \"memory market stays tight: demand above supply, prices sharply higher, new industry capacity not online until 2027 or later\",\n    \"period\": \"until 2028\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e152\",\n    \"summary\": \"The author estimates that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 2,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"the article's author\",\n   \"passage\": \"new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"the article's author\",\n    \"what\": \"new memory capacity comes online only in 2027 or later, so memory supply stays low\",\n    \"period\": \"at least the next year and a half\",\n    \"status\": \"view with a reason\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e152\",\n    \"summary\": \"The author estimates that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 3,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-07\",\n   \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\",\n   \"who\": \"Micron (guidance, as reported by the author)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Micron\",\n    \"what\": \"revenue guidance\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$50bn\",\n    \"period\": \"Micron's next fiscal quarter\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast (guidance)\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:d0f3f83296df884fae64921e#e152\",\n    \"summary\": \"The author estimates that AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data-center capital expenditures in calendar 2026.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 4,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"How Nvidia Became The Black Sheep Of The Chip Stock Rally\",\n   \"who\": \"Micron (guidance, as cited)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Micron guidance\",\n    \"what\": \"adjusted gross margin (memory pricing power)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"86%\",\n    \"period\": \"next quarter\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e88\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion in the quarter discussed.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Revenue rose 85% year over year to $81.6 billion\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e89\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA data-center revenue rose 92% year over year to $75.2 billion in the quarter discussed.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"data center up 92% to $75.2 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e90\",\n    \"summary\": \"Reports said NVIDIA's upcoming Vera Rubin server systems were delayed; the item does not specify the extent or timing.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e91\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said agentic AI was doing productive work, generating real value and scaling across companies and industries.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"CEO Jensen Huang framed it plainly: “Agentic AI has arrived, doing productive work, generating real value and scaling rapidly across companies and industries.”\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e92\",\n    \"summary\": \"The author estimated NVIDIA's share of the AI chip market at roughly 81%.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVIDIA still owns roughly 81% of the AI chip market\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e93\",\n    \"summary\": \"Broadcom guided to Q3 AI semiconductor revenue of $16.0 billion, up more than 200% year over year.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Broadcom guided Q3 AI semiconductor revenue to $16.0 billion, more than 200% year over year.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e94\",\n    \"summary\": \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecast custom ASICs would grow at a 27% CAGR through 2033, versus 16% for AI accelerators.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Bloomberg Intelligence forecasting a 27% CAGR for custom ASICs through 2033 versus 16% for AI accelerators.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e95\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron Q3 revenue rose 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Q3 revenue exploded 345.7% year over year to $41.46 billion\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e96\",\n    \"summary\": \"Micron forecast an adjusted gross margin of 86% next quarter.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Micron is now forecasting adjusted gross margins of 86% next quarter\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32#e97\",\n    \"summary\": \"Some estimates put memory at half of total data-center spending in 2027.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Some estimates put half of all data center spend going toward memory in 2027.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 5,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-08\",\n   \"title\": \"China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\",\n   \"who\": \"The Information, citing two unidentified people (via Bloomberg / Straits Times)\",\n   \"passage\": \"China plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Chinese officials\",\n    \"what\": \"permission for top AI companies (Alibaba, ByteDance, DeepSeek) to buy NVIDIA H200 chips, each on approval stating the number and reason\",\n    \"how_much\": \"a limited amount; total may be fewer than 200,000\",\n    \"condition\": \"approval per request; limited amount\",\n    \"status\": \"rumour\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [],\n  \"note\": \"the run kept nothing from this article\"\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 6,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n   \"who\": \"China's government (stated by Tom's Hardware)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Beijing\",\n    \"what\": \"Chinese companies not allowed to buy NVIDIA H200\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e421\",\n    \"summary\": \"Unnamed sources cited by the Financial Times said NVIDIA cut its roster of authorized Asian customers by more than half and used tougher inspections, including customer-site visits, contract checks, and end-user interviews.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e422\",\n    \"summary\": \"Tom's Hardware said the US and allied crackdown on AI GPU smuggling had reduced AI GPU supply in China, making it harder for Chinese AI companies to obtain processors.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Washington’s and its allies’ crackdown on AI GPU smuggling have cut supply in China, which is now making it harder for AI companies to procure the processors they need.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e423\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article said Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA H200 GPUs that the US had permitted NVIDIA to export to select regional customers.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e424\",\n    \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that Chinese domestic semiconductor suppliers were sold out and companies were considering less powerful chips instead.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 7,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n   \"who\": \"an unnamed tech executive (to the Financial Times)\",\n   \"passage\": \"all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"unnamed Chinese tech executive\",\n    \"what\": \"China's domestic AI-chip suppliers are sold out; buyers are considering less powerful chips\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e421\",\n    \"summary\": \"Unnamed sources cited by the Financial Times said NVIDIA cut its roster of authorized Asian customers by more than half and used tougher inspections, including customer-site visits, contract checks, and end-user interviews.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e422\",\n    \"summary\": \"Tom's Hardware said the US and allied crackdown on AI GPU smuggling had reduced AI GPU supply in China, making it harder for Chinese AI companies to obtain processors.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Washington’s and its allies’ crackdown on AI GPU smuggling have cut supply in China, which is now making it harder for AI companies to procure the processors they need.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e423\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article said Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA H200 GPUs that the US had permitted NVIDIA to export to select regional customers.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e424\",\n    \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that Chinese domestic semiconductor suppliers were sold out and companies were considering less powerful chips instead.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 8,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-14\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\",\n   \"who\": \"US President (as reported)\",\n   \"passage\": \"President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"US President Trump\",\n    \"what\": \"allowed H200 exports to select customers in China\",\n    \"period\": \"December 2025\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e421\",\n    \"summary\": \"Unnamed sources cited by the Financial Times said NVIDIA cut its roster of authorized Asian customers by more than half and used tougher inspections, including customer-site visits, contract checks, and end-user interviews.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e422\",\n    \"summary\": \"Tom's Hardware said the US and allied crackdown on AI GPU smuggling had reduced AI GPU supply in China, making it harder for Chinese AI companies to obtain processors.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Washington’s and its allies’ crackdown on AI GPU smuggling have cut supply in China, which is now making it harder for AI companies to procure the processors they need.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e423\",\n    \"summary\": \"The article said Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy NVIDIA H200 GPUs that the US had permitted NVIDIA to export to select regional customers.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:2a41a34c16f5280b624481fb#e424\",\n    \"summary\": \"An unnamed tech executive told the Financial Times that Chinese domestic semiconductor suppliers were sold out and companies were considering less powerful chips instead.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 9,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Sharon AI (counterparty disclosure, reported by SemiAnalysis)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Sharon AI disclosed\",\n    \"what\": \"total value of NVIDIA's backstop on Sharon's 72MW Australian AI factory (up to 40,000 GB300)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$4.88B; implied floor ~$2.33/hr/GPU\",\n    \"period\": \"six years\",\n    \"condition\": \"up to 40,000 GB300s\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\",\n    \"status_note\": \"announced June 2026\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e59\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates NVIDIA’s second-half 2026 shipment volumes and revenue above general-market estimates, based on its supply-chain tracking.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e60\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started providing neoclouds take-or-pay minimum-revenue guarantees on GPU capacity, in return for a share of revenue above the guarantee.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e61\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping data-center leases as part of its support for neoclouds.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e62\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA’s backstops typically run for six years; its illustrative lower-end pricing curve averages $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, and it expects most neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e63\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis models a 25.4% project IRR for a neocloud’s one-year rental-book scenario with an NVIDIA backstop, the highest return among the backstopped scenarios it analyzed.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We see that the short-term 1y rental book scenario clearly offers the best IRR among the backstopped scenarios at 25.4%, and these IRRs could realize even higher if the rental market turns out to be strong enough such that 1y and shorter rental rates decay by less than we modeled in the curve below.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e64\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says banks underwriting NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing seek at least 1.3x debt-service coverage, typically corresponding to 70–80% loan-to-value, especially in the first years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e65\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis expects initial lending for NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing to price wider than hyperscaler-backstopped deals at about 5.9% yield but tighter than CoreWeave’s roughly 10% unsecured bond yield.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We think that initial lending will be at a wider spread than the current 5y backstopped hyperscale deals which come in at around SOFR + 225bps (roughly equivalent to a Z-spread of 195bps, or about 5.9% total yield) but at a tighter spread than CoreWeave’s 5-year unsecured corporate bond which has a yield of ~10% (roughly equivalent to Z-spread of 600bps).\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e66\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced a 72MW Australian AI factory backed by NVIDIA for up to 40,000 GB300 GPUs over six years, disclosing $4.88 billion of backstop value, or an implied average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e67\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI guides to a total fleet of more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs by mid-2027, including up to 40,000 backstopped GB300s; its footprint is set to reach 132MW, of which 102MW is contracted.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e68\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced its 360MW Batam AI cluster project on June 29, 2026, as part of NVIDIA’s backstop program.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e69\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years for the Batam cluster project.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e70\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Beyond this – Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e71\",\n    \"summary\": \"CoreWeave paid a 5.9% yield on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5 billion DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backed by Meta, about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s roughly 5.0% five-year bond yield; CoreWeave’s five-year unsecured bonds yielded about 10%.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 10,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Firmus (announced June 29, 2026; reported by SemiAnalysis)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n    \"what\": \"a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster\",\n    \"how_much\": \"360MW\",\n    \"period\": \"announced June 29, 2026\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\",\n    \"status_note\": \"announced\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e59\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates NVIDIA’s second-half 2026 shipment volumes and revenue above general-market estimates, based on its supply-chain tracking.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e60\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started providing neoclouds take-or-pay minimum-revenue guarantees on GPU capacity, in return for a share of revenue above the guarantee.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e61\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping data-center leases as part of its support for neoclouds.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e62\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA’s backstops typically run for six years; its illustrative lower-end pricing curve averages $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, and it expects most neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e63\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis models a 25.4% project IRR for a neocloud’s one-year rental-book scenario with an NVIDIA backstop, the highest return among the backstopped scenarios it analyzed.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We see that the short-term 1y rental book scenario clearly offers the best IRR among the backstopped scenarios at 25.4%, and these IRRs could realize even higher if the rental market turns out to be strong enough such that 1y and shorter rental rates decay by less than we modeled in the curve below.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e64\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says banks underwriting NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing seek at least 1.3x debt-service coverage, typically corresponding to 70–80% loan-to-value, especially in the first years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e65\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis expects initial lending for NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing to price wider than hyperscaler-backstopped deals at about 5.9% yield but tighter than CoreWeave’s roughly 10% unsecured bond yield.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We think that initial lending will be at a wider spread than the current 5y backstopped hyperscale deals which come in at around SOFR + 225bps (roughly equivalent to a Z-spread of 195bps, or about 5.9% total yield) but at a tighter spread than CoreWeave’s 5-year unsecured corporate bond which has a yield of ~10% (roughly equivalent to Z-spread of 600bps).\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e66\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced a 72MW Australian AI factory backed by NVIDIA for up to 40,000 GB300 GPUs over six years, disclosing $4.88 billion of backstop value, or an implied average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e67\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI guides to a total fleet of more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs by mid-2027, including up to 40,000 backstopped GB300s; its footprint is set to reach 132MW, of which 102MW is contracted.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e68\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced its 360MW Batam AI cluster project on June 29, 2026, as part of NVIDIA’s backstop program.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e69\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years for the Batam cluster project.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e70\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Beyond this – Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e71\",\n    \"summary\": \"CoreWeave paid a 5.9% yield on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5 billion DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backed by Meta, about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s roughly 5.0% five-year bond yield; CoreWeave’s five-year unsecured bonds yielded about 10%.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 11,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n   \"passage\": \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"Firmus\",\n    \"what\": \"expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)\",\n    \"how_much\": \"$25B-$30B\",\n    \"period\": \"over six years\",\n    \"status\": \"forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e59\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates NVIDIA’s second-half 2026 shipment volumes and revenue above general-market estimates, based on its supply-chain tracking.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e60\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started providing neoclouds take-or-pay minimum-revenue guarantees on GPU capacity, in return for a share of revenue above the guarantee.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e61\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping data-center leases as part of its support for neoclouds.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e62\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA’s backstops typically run for six years; its illustrative lower-end pricing curve averages $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, and it expects most neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e63\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis models a 25.4% project IRR for a neocloud’s one-year rental-book scenario with an NVIDIA backstop, the highest return among the backstopped scenarios it analyzed.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We see that the short-term 1y rental book scenario clearly offers the best IRR among the backstopped scenarios at 25.4%, and these IRRs could realize even higher if the rental market turns out to be strong enough such that 1y and shorter rental rates decay by less than we modeled in the curve below.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e64\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says banks underwriting NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing seek at least 1.3x debt-service coverage, typically corresponding to 70–80% loan-to-value, especially in the first years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e65\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis expects initial lending for NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing to price wider than hyperscaler-backstopped deals at about 5.9% yield but tighter than CoreWeave’s roughly 10% unsecured bond yield.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We think that initial lending will be at a wider spread than the current 5y backstopped hyperscale deals which come in at around SOFR + 225bps (roughly equivalent to a Z-spread of 195bps, or about 5.9% total yield) but at a tighter spread than CoreWeave’s 5-year unsecured corporate bond which has a yield of ~10% (roughly equivalent to Z-spread of 600bps).\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e66\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced a 72MW Australian AI factory backed by NVIDIA for up to 40,000 GB300 GPUs over six years, disclosing $4.88 billion of backstop value, or an implied average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e67\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI guides to a total fleet of more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs by mid-2027, including up to 40,000 backstopped GB300s; its footprint is set to reach 132MW, of which 102MW is contracted.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e68\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced its 360MW Batam AI cluster project on June 29, 2026, as part of NVIDIA’s backstop program.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e69\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years for the Batam cluster project.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e70\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Beyond this – Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e71\",\n    \"summary\": \"CoreWeave paid a 5.9% yield on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5 billion DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backed by Meta, about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s roughly 5.0% five-year bond yield; CoreWeave’s five-year unsecured bonds yielded about 10%.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 12,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\",\n    \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e59\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates NVIDIA’s second-half 2026 shipment volumes and revenue above general-market estimates, based on its supply-chain tracking.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e60\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started providing neoclouds take-or-pay minimum-revenue guarantees on GPU capacity, in return for a share of revenue above the guarantee.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e61\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping data-center leases as part of its support for neoclouds.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e62\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA’s backstops typically run for six years; its illustrative lower-end pricing curve averages $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, and it expects most neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e63\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis models a 25.4% project IRR for a neocloud’s one-year rental-book scenario with an NVIDIA backstop, the highest return among the backstopped scenarios it analyzed.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We see that the short-term 1y rental book scenario clearly offers the best IRR among the backstopped scenarios at 25.4%, and these IRRs could realize even higher if the rental market turns out to be strong enough such that 1y and shorter rental rates decay by less than we modeled in the curve below.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e64\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says banks underwriting NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing seek at least 1.3x debt-service coverage, typically corresponding to 70–80% loan-to-value, especially in the first years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e65\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis expects initial lending for NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing to price wider than hyperscaler-backstopped deals at about 5.9% yield but tighter than CoreWeave’s roughly 10% unsecured bond yield.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We think that initial lending will be at a wider spread than the current 5y backstopped hyperscale deals which come in at around SOFR + 225bps (roughly equivalent to a Z-spread of 195bps, or about 5.9% total yield) but at a tighter spread than CoreWeave’s 5-year unsecured corporate bond which has a yield of ~10% (roughly equivalent to Z-spread of 600bps).\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e66\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced a 72MW Australian AI factory backed by NVIDIA for up to 40,000 GB300 GPUs over six years, disclosing $4.88 billion of backstop value, or an implied average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e67\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI guides to a total fleet of more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs by mid-2027, including up to 40,000 backstopped GB300s; its footprint is set to reach 132MW, of which 102MW is contracted.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e68\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced its 360MW Batam AI cluster project on June 29, 2026, as part of NVIDIA’s backstop program.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e69\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years for the Batam cluster project.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e70\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Beyond this – Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e71\",\n    \"summary\": \"CoreWeave paid a 5.9% yield on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5 billion DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backed by Meta, about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s roughly 5.0% five-year bond yield; CoreWeave’s five-year unsecured bonds yielded about 10%.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 13,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"who\": \"The Information (prior reporting)\",\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\",\n    \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\",\n    \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\",\n    \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\",\n    \"status\": \"rumour\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information reported on 2026-07-01 that NVIDIA was providing young GPU cloud providers financial guarantees, including repurchasing their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price if they failed to secure enough customers.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced on 2026-07-01 a business model combining revenue sharing and credit support for AI cloud providers procuring NVIDIA infrastructure.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Under the reported arrangements, NVIDIA will receive a declining percentage share of participating cloud providers’ revenue over the contract term.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus and Sharon AI have joined NVIDIA’s reported AI cloud-provider program, according to The Information and three executives with existing NVIDIA business ties.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, planned to scale to up to 360 MW and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e8\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information previously reported that NVIDIA was negotiating to guarantee OpenAI’s lease of a large Ohio data center, whose fully built-out cost could reach $500 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e9\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA disclosed in a filing submitted in May 2026 that it had added $3.5 billion of guarantees for customer data-center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares; the filing covered the quarter ended in April 2026.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 14,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (post on Monday)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (a research firm's report, not NVIDIA)\",\n    \"what\": \"Kyber NVL144 rack for Vera Rubin Ultra delayed; cause: PCB midplane manufacturability\",\n    \"how_much\": \"more than 12 months\",\n    \"period\": \"from 2027 to 2028\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e47\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed for Rubin Ultra, had been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e48\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis’s report and said its product roadmap remained intact.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e49\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture was delayed to 2028 because its PCB midplane remained difficult to manufacture.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e50\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA’s NVL576 system, which links eight racks, was likely to be delayed or produced in only small volumes.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e51\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA scrapped its backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together after cloud providers and hyperscalers objected to the design and operating burden.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e52\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA had no proven solution to expand Rubin Ultra’s scale-up system size and that this could give AMD and Google a technical opening in the high-end market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e53\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems were in full production and were scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e54\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 would be 20% above Wall Street consensus.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 15,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\",\n   \"passage\": \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"four-die Rubin Ultra cancelled; two-die version remains\",\n    \"how_much\": \"roughly half the real-world compute\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\",\n    \"status_note\": \"cancelled\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e116\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA had canceled the four-compute-die version of its upcoming Rubin Ultra chip.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e117\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said only the two-die Rubin Ultra version remained, with roughly half the real-world compute performance of the canceled four-die version.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e118\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology would not arrive until the Feynman generation, the generation after next.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e119\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA currently lacked a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e120\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said the gap in NVIDIA's ability to scale Rubin Ultra could give AMD's MI500X and Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to compete.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e121\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA planned to offset the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e122\",\n    \"summary\": \"Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments said the reported Kyber delay did not necessarily mean overall AI spending would shrink.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A Kyber delay doesn't necessarily mean overall AI spending will shrink, said Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments, according to Bloomberg .\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 16,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis (as reported)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis, about NVIDIA's plan\",\n    \"what\": \"sell more Oberon-Rubin racks to cover the Kyber gap\",\n    \"status\": \"plan\",\n    \"status_note\": \"as claimed\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e116\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA had canceled the four-compute-die version of its upcoming Rubin Ultra chip.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e117\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said only the two-die Rubin Ultra version remained, with roughly half the real-world compute performance of the canceled four-die version.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e118\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA's CPO-NVSwitch interconnect technology would not arrive until the Feynman generation, the generation after next.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e119\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA currently lacked a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e120\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said the gap in NVIDIA's ability to scale Rubin Ultra could give AMD's MI500X and Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to compete.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e121\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA planned to offset the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:795ea94e1100415a67fd1a96#e122\",\n    \"summary\": \"Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments said the reported Kyber delay did not necessarily mean overall AI spending would shrink.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A Kyber delay doesn't necessarily mean overall AI spending will shrink, said Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments, according to Bloomberg .\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 17,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA (statement to CNBC)\",\n   \"passage\": \"Our roadmap is intact\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"what\": \"rejects the SemiAnalysis report; says its roadmap (Rubin Ultra / Kyber in 2027) is unchanged\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e47\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reported that NVIDIA’s Kyber rack-scale architecture, designed for Rubin Ultra, had been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e48\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA rejected SemiAnalysis’s report and said its product roadmap remained intact.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e49\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said the Kyber NVL144 rack architecture was delayed to 2028 because its PCB midplane remained difficult to manufacture.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e50\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA’s NVL576 system, which links eight racks, was likely to be delayed or produced in only small volumes.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"NVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e51\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA scrapped its backup plan to bolt two current-generation racks together after cloud providers and hyperscalers objected to the design and operating burden.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"A backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e52\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis said NVIDIA had no proven solution to expand Rubin Ultra’s scale-up system size and that this could give AMD and Google a technical opening in the high-end market.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"That leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e53\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems were in full production and were scheduled to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587#e54\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis projected NVIDIA data-center compute revenue in the second half of FY2027 would be 20% above Wall Street consensus.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 18,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n   \"passage\": \"An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA representative\",\n    \"what\": \"denies the report: roadmap intact\",\n    \"condition\": \"unclear whether the original or an already updated roadmap is meant\",\n    \"status\": \"claim\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [],\n  \"note\": \"the run kept nothing from this article\"\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 19,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n   \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n   \"who\": \"NVIDIA (May 2026 quarterly filing)\",\n   \"passage\": \"NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n    \"what\": \"guarantees of customer data center leases, in exchange for rights to buy their shares\",\n    \"how_much\": \"an additional $3.5 billion\",\n    \"period\": \"quarter ended April 2026 (filed May)\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e2\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information reported on 2026-07-01 that NVIDIA was providing young GPU cloud providers financial guarantees, including repurchasing their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price if they failed to secure enough customers.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e3\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA announced on 2026-07-01 a business model combining revenue sharing and credit support for AI cloud providers procuring NVIDIA infrastructure.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e4\",\n    \"summary\": \"Under the reported arrangements, NVIDIA will receive a declining percentage share of participating cloud providers’ revenue over the contract term.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"In return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e5\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus and Sharon AI have joined NVIDIA’s reported AI cloud-provider program, according to The Information and three executives with existing NVIDIA business ties.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e6\",\n    \"summary\": \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e7\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, planned to scale to up to 360 MW and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e8\",\n    \"summary\": \"The Information previously reported that NVIDIA was negotiating to guarantee OpenAI’s lease of a large Ohio data center, whose fully built-out cost could reach $500 billion.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45#e9\",\n    \"summary\": \"NVIDIA disclosed in a filing submitted in May 2026 that it had added $3.5 billion of guarantees for customer data-center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares; the filing covered the quarter ended in April 2026.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"point\": 20,\n  \"decisive_item\": {\n   \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n   \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n   \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n   \"passage\": \"Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases\",\n   \"meaning\": {\n    \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n    \"what\": \"NVIDIA backstops datacenter leases for neoclouds (a second kind of obligation)\",\n    \"status\": \"happened\"\n   }\n  },\n  \"kept_from_that_article\": [\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e59\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis estimates NVIDIA’s second-half 2026 shipment volumes and revenue above general-market estimates, based on its supply-chain tracking.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e60\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started providing neoclouds take-or-pay minimum-revenue guarantees on GPU capacity, in return for a share of revenue above the guarantee.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e61\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping data-center leases as part of its support for neoclouds.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e62\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA’s backstops typically run for six years; its illustrative lower-end pricing curve averages $2.36 per GPU-hour over six years, and it expects most neoclouds to negotiate higher backstops.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e63\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis models a 25.4% project IRR for a neocloud’s one-year rental-book scenario with an NVIDIA backstop, the highest return among the backstopped scenarios it analyzed.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We see that the short-term 1y rental book scenario clearly offers the best IRR among the backstopped scenarios at 25.4%, and these IRRs could realize even higher if the rental market turns out to be strong enough such that 1y and shorter rental rates decay by less than we modeled in the curve below.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e64\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis says banks underwriting NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing seek at least 1.3x debt-service coverage, typically corresponding to 70–80% loan-to-value, especially in the first years.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e65\",\n    \"summary\": \"SemiAnalysis expects initial lending for NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing to price wider than hyperscaler-backstopped deals at about 5.9% yield but tighter than CoreWeave’s roughly 10% unsecured bond yield.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"We think that initial lending will be at a wider spread than the current 5y backstopped hyperscale deals which come in at around SOFR + 225bps (roughly equivalent to a Z-spread of 195bps, or about 5.9% total yield) but at a tighter spread than CoreWeave’s 5-year unsecured corporate bond which has a yield of ~10% (roughly equivalent to Z-spread of 600bps).\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e66\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI announced a 72MW Australian AI factory backed by NVIDIA for up to 40,000 GB300 GPUs over six years, disclosing $4.88 billion of backstop value, or an implied average floor of about $2.33 per GPU-hour.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e67\",\n    \"summary\": \"SharonAI guides to a total fleet of more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs by mid-2027, including up to 40,000 backstopped GB300s; its footprint is set to reach 132MW, of which 102MW is contracted.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e68\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced its 360MW Batam AI cluster project on June 29, 2026, as part of NVIDIA’s backstop program.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e69\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus expects $25 billion to $30 billion of customer revenue over six years for the Batam cluster project.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e70\",\n    \"summary\": \"Firmus announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Beyond this – Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032.\"\n    ]\n   },\n   {\n    \"ref\": \"gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6#e71\",\n    \"summary\": \"CoreWeave paid a 5.9% yield on the fixed-rate tranche of its $8.5 billion DDTL 4.0 delayed-draw term loan backed by Meta, about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s roughly 5.0% five-year bond yield; CoreWeave’s five-year unsecured bonds yielded about 10%.\",\n    \"passages\": [\n     \"Readers may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%.\"\n    ]\n   }\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
